<!-- Section 1: Introduction -->
<section id="sec-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate the Area of a Right Triangle Using Its Hypotenuse and Altitude</h2>
  <p>A right triangle has a hypotenuse of <span set:html={rich('[[10\\text{ m}]]')} /> and the altitude drawn to that hypotenuse measures <span set:html={rich('[[4.8\\text{ m}]]')} />. Apply <span set:html={rich('[[K=ch/2]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[K=(10\\times4.8)/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=24\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} />. The triangle encloses 24 square metres of flat area. This is the complete method the calculator automates: one side, its perpendicular height, one multiplication, one halving.</p>
  <p>The hypotenuse works as the base because any side of a triangle can be the base when the matching perpendicular height is known. The altitude is the perpendicular segment from the right-angle vertex to the hypotenuse, and it must meet the hypotenuse at a right angle; a slanted segment is not the height this formula needs. Since the area depends only on the base and its height, the two legs are not required as inputs, and the result is always expressed in the square units of the length unit used.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: Understanding the geometry -->
<section id="sec-understand" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Hypotenuse, Legs, and Altitude</h2>
  <p>The notation throughout is: <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[C]]')} /> are the vertices and angles with <span set:html={rich('[[C=90^\\circ]]')} />; <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> are the two legs; <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the hypotenuse opposite <span set:html={rich('[[C]]')} />; <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /> is the perpendicular altitude from vertex <span set:html={rich('[[C]]')} /> to the hypotenuse; <span set:html={rich('[[D]]')} /> is where the altitude meets the hypotenuse; <span set:html={rich('[[x]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[y]]')} /> are the two hypotenuse segments; and <span set:html={rich('[[K]]')} /> is the area, used everywhere so it is never confused with angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /></p>

  <h3>What Is a Right Triangle?</h3>
  <p>A right triangle contains exactly one 90-degree angle. Its two legs meet at that angle, and the hypotenuse lies opposite it as the longest side. The other two angles are acute, and since the three angles sum to 180 degrees, <span set:html={rich('[[A+B+C=180^\\circ]]')} /> with <span set:html={rich('[[C=90^\\circ]]')} /> gives <span set:html={rich('[[A+B=90^\\circ]]')} />. The altitude method depends on this: the base is the longest side, the height drops from the right-angle corner.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Hypotenuse?</h3>
  <p>The hypotenuse is the side opposite the right angle, labelled <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> in the calculator. To find it in any rotation, locate the side that does not touch the square right-angle marker; that side is the hypotenuse. It is longer than either leg and need not be drawn horizontally: a base is a chosen reference side, not a screen direction.</p>

  <h3>What Is an Altitude in Geometry?</h3>
  <p>An altitude is a perpendicular line segment drawn from a vertex to the line containing the opposite side. It must form a 90-degree angle with that side, which is what makes it a height rather than just a line. An altitude is not necessarily one of the triangle's original sides, and which segment counts as the altitude changes with the chosen base.</p>

  <h3>Understanding the Altitude to the Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>In triangle <span set:html={rich('[[ABC]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[C]]')} /> is the right-angle vertex and <span set:html={rich('[[AB]]')} /> is the hypotenuse. The altitude <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /> is the segment <span set:html={rich('[[CD]]')} /> drawn from <span set:html={rich('[[C]]')} /> perpendicular to <span set:html={rich('[[AB]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[CD\\perp AB]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[CD=h]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[AB=c]]')} />, with <span set:html={rich('[[D]]')} /> as the foot, splitting the triangle into two smaller right triangles nested inside it.</p>

  <h3>How the Altitude Divides the Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>Let the altitude cut the hypotenuse into <span set:html={rich('[[AD=x]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[DB=y]]')} />. Then <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{x+y=c}]]]')} />. Each smaller triangle contains a right angle and shares one acute angle with the original, so all three triangles are similar. Similarity of the two smaller triangles gives <span set:html={rich('[[x/h=h/y]]')} />, and cross-multiplying yields <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{h^2=xy}]]]')} />, the geometric mean relationship: the altitude is the geometric mean of the two hypotenuse segments, and the starting point for the maximum-altitude rule below.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('altitude-diagram.svg')} alt="Triangle ABC with right angle at C, hypotenuse AB labeled c, altitude CD labeled h, segments x and y" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Triangle ABC with the altitude CD to the hypotenuse: AD = x, DB = y, x + y = c, h&sup2; = xy, and K = ch/2.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>
<!-- Section 3: Deriving the formula -->
<section id="sec-formula" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Is the Area Formula Half the Hypotenuse Times the Altitude?</h2>
  <p>Every triangle's area starts from one rule <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac12\\times\\text{base}\\times\\text{perpendicular height}]]')} />. Any side may be the base if the height used is perpendicular to it. This calculator chooses the hypotenuse as the base, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\text{base}=c]]')} /> and the matching perpendicular height is the altitude, <span set:html={rich('[[\\text{height}=h]]')} />. Substituting gives <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac12\\times c\\times h]]')} />, which is <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\frac{ch}{2}}]]]')} /></p>

  <h3>Why Can the Hypotenuse Be Used as the Base?</h3>
  <p>The hypotenuse is simply the selected base for this calculation. The altitude must be measured from the opposite vertex, the right-angle vertex <span set:html={rich('[[C]]')} />, and it must be perpendicular to the hypotenuse. Choosing a different side as the base with its own matching altitude gives the same area. The base is a role a side plays, not a permanent label.</p>

  <h3>Why Is the Formula Divided by Two?</h3>
  <p>A triangle fills exactly half of the parallelogram built on the same base with the same perpendicular height. With base 10 and height 4.8, the parallelogram's area is <span set:html={rich('[[10\\times4.8=48]]')} />; the triangle inside it occupies half, so <span set:html={rich('[[K=48/2=24]]')} />. Skipping the halving answers a different question: the parallelogram's area, not the triangle's.</p>

  <h3>Does the Formula Work Only for Right Triangles?</h3>
  <p>The equation <span set:html={rich('[[K=ch/2]]')} /> is the general triangle area formula, valid for any triangle whenever <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the chosen base and <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /> is its perpendicular altitude. What makes this page specific to right triangles is the reading: <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the hypotenuse, <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /> drops from the right-angle vertex, and the altitude obeys the extra restriction <span set:html={rich('[[h\\leq c/2]]')} />, which has no meaning for general triangles.</p>

  <h3>Why You Do Not Need Both Legs</h3>
  <p>Area needs one side and its corresponding perpendicular height, nothing more. When <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /> are already known, the legs add no information that changes the area. The legs formula <span set:html={rich('[[K=ab/2]]')} /> is a different input route to the same quantity, worth using when the measured data are the two perpendicular sides. If the legs are what you have, the <a href="/right-triangle-area-from-legs-calculator/">Right Triangle Area From Legs Calculator</a> takes them directly.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('base-height-comparison.svg')} alt="Comparison of area K = ch/2 using hypotenuse and altitude versus K = ab/2 using the two legs" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Two routes to the same area: the hypotenuse with its altitude, and the two legs. Both equal the enclosed region, so ch/2 = ab/2.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: Relationship between hypotenuse, altitude, and legs -->
<section id="sec-relationship" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How the Hypotenuse and Altitude Relate to the Two Legs</h2>
  <p>Two valid area formulas describe the same triangle: <span set:html={rich('[[K=ab/2]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[K=ch/2]]')} />. Since they calculate the same area, <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{ab}{2}=\\frac{ch}{2}]]')} />. Multiply both sides by two: <span set:html={rich('[[ab=ch]]')} />. Rearranging for the altitude gives <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{h=\\frac{ab}{c}}]]]')} />. The altitude to the hypotenuse is the product of the two legs divided by the hypotenuse, which explains why the two area formulas can never disagree on a consistent triangle.</p>

  <h3>Why This Relationship Is Useful</h3>
  <p>It connects three ways of describing the same geometry. The legs and hypotenuse determine the altitude, so a triangle measured by its sides already contains it. The altitude and hypotenuse determine the area, so the area never needs the legs to be recovered first. Computing the area through either formula and comparing is one of the strongest checks, because a mistake in one route rarely reproduces itself in the other.</p>

  <h3>Connecting the Formula With the Pythagorean Theorem</h3>
  <p>The Pythagorean theorem <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]')} /> verifies the link between the legs and the hypotenuse. Take a 3-4-5 triangle: <span set:html={rich('[[3^2+4^2=5^2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[9+16=25]]')} />. The altitude is <span set:html={rich('[[h=\\frac{3\\times4}{5}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{h=2.4}]]]')} />. The area from hypotenuse and altitude is <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{5\\times2.4}{2}=6]]')} />, and from the legs it is <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{3\\times4}{2}=6]]')} />. Both formulas measure the same enclosed region, and <span set:html={rich('[[h=ab/c]]')} /> is the link that guarantees it.</p>

  <h3>How the Altitude Creates Two Similar Triangles</h3>
  <p>The altitude divides the original triangle into two smaller right triangles, each sharing its acute angles with the original. Two shared angles make all three triangles similar, so corresponding sides stay in proportion. Matching the altitude against the hypotenuse segments gives <span set:html={rich('[[x/h=h/y]]')} />, which cross-multiplies to <span set:html={rich('[[h^2=xy]]')} />: the geometric mean relationship is similarity written as an equation.</p>
</section>
<!-- Section 5: Maximum altitude -->
<section id="sec-max" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Can the Altitude Never Be Longer Than Half the Hypotenuse?</h2>
  <p>Every valid right triangle obeys <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{h\\leq\\frac{c}{2}}]]]')} />. This is a geometric fact, and it deserves a derivation.</p>

  <h3>Understanding the Two Hypotenuse Segments</h3>
  <p>Recall <span set:html={rich('[[x+y=c]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[h^2=xy]]')} />. The product <span set:html={rich('[[xy]]')} /> is largest when the two segments are equal, a consequence of the arithmetic-geometric mean inequality: <span set:html={rich('[[xy\\leq\\left(\\frac{x+y}{2}\\right)^2]]')} />. Since <span set:html={rich('[[x+y=c]]')} />, the right side becomes <span set:html={rich('[[c^2/4]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[h^2\\leq\\frac{c^2}{4}]]')} />, and for positive lengths <span set:html={rich('[[h\\leq c/2]]')} />. In plain terms: unequal splits shrink the product and the altitude; the tallest altitude comes from the most even split.</p>

  <h3>What Happens at the Maximum Altitude?</h3>
  <p>The maximum occurs when <span set:html={rich('[[x=y=c/2]]')} />, and then <span set:html={rich('[[h=c/2]]')} />. This produces an isosceles right triangle: its two legs are equal, and its acute angles are <span set:html={rich('[[45^\\circ]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[45^\\circ]]')} />. The altitude then doubles as the median to the hypotenuse, a valid triangle, not a degenerate one.</p>

  <h3>Example of Maximum Altitude</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} />, the maximum altitude is <span set:html={rich('[[h_{\\max}=10/2=5]]')} />. The maximum area is <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{10\\times5}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K_{\\max}=25}]]]')} />. No right triangle with hypotenuse 10 can have an altitude above 5, and therefore none can have an area above 25 square units.</p>

  <h3>What Happens When Altitude Is Greater Than c/2?</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[h=5.5]]')} />, the maximum valid altitude is 5, so these measurements cannot describe a right triangle. The formula still produces <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{10\\times5.5}{2}=27.5]]')} />, but that number represents no valid right triangle with those measurements. Treat it as a signal that a measurement is wrong, not as an area. This guide makes no claim about whether the calculator rejects such inputs automatically.</p>

  <h3>What Happens When Altitude Is Much Smaller?</h3>
  <p>A small altitude relative to the hypotenuse describes a narrow, elongated right triangle with one leg much longer than the other. With the hypotenuse fixed, the area decreases as the altitude decreases, because <span set:html={rich('[[K=ch/2]]')} /> scales directly with <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} />. There is no positive lower bound: the altitude can approach zero, and the triangle flattens toward a degenerate line.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('max-altitude.svg')} alt="Maximum altitude c/2 in an isosceles right triangle, a valid smaller altitude, and an invalid case" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Left: maximum altitude at c/2 gives the largest area. Middle: a smaller valid altitude. Right: an altitude above c/2 cannot belong to a right triangle.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 6: How to use the calculator -->
<section id="sec-howto" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the Right Triangle Area From Hypotenuse and Altitude Calculator</h2>
  <p>The calculator needs exactly two inputs. The walkthrough below follows the on-screen order, from a measured triangle to a verified result.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Measure or identify the hypotenuse.</strong> The longest side, opposite the 90-degree angle.</li>
    <li><strong>Identify the perpendicular altitude drawn to the hypotenuse.</strong> It starts at the right-angle vertex and meets the hypotenuse at 90 degrees.</li>
    <li><strong>Confirm the altitude is perpendicular.</strong> A segment measured along a sloping side is not the altitude the formula needs.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Enter the altitude <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} />.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Select compatible length units.</strong> Both measurements must use the same unit.</li>
    <li><strong>Check that both measurements are positive.</strong> Zero or negative lengths describe no triangle.</li>
    <li><strong>Confirm that <span set:html={rich('[[h\\leq c/2]]')} />.</strong> For <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} />, any altitude above 5 cannot belong to a right triangle with that hypotenuse.</li>
    <li><strong>Click Calculate Area.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Review the result.</strong> The area appears in the square units matching the inputs.</li>
    <li><strong>Check the square units.</strong> Metres give square metres; centimetres give square centimetres.</li>
    <li><strong>Review the solution steps.</strong> They show the substitution and the arithmetic.</li>
    <li><strong>Use Reset before entering a new triangle.</strong> It clears both fields so the next calculation starts clean.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The calculator does not ask for the two legs or an angle: one side and its matching perpendicular height determine the area completely.</p>
</section>
<!-- Section 7: Four foundational worked examples -->
<section id="sec-examples" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Examples of Right Triangle Area Using Hypotenuse and Altitude</h2>
  <p>Four worked examples build the method from the familiar upward. Each one applies <span set:html={rich('[[K=ch/2]]')} /> with every substitution shown, and each one checks that the altitude respects the right-triangle limit.</p>

  <h3>Example 1: Using the Existing Calculator's Example</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[h=4.8]]')} />. Step 1: write the formula, <span set:html={rich('[[K=ch/2]]')} />. Step 2: substitute, <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{10\\times4.8}{2}]]')} />. Step 3: multiply, <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{48}{2}]]')} />. Final answer: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=24\\text{ square units}}]]]')} />. The altitude is valid because <span set:html={rich('[[4.8<5]]')} />, below the maximum of half the hypotenuse.</p>

  <h3>Example 2: A 3-4-5 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />. First find the altitude: <span set:html={rich('[[h=ab/c]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[h=\\frac{3\\times4}{5}=2.4]]')} />. Then <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{5\\times2.4}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=6}]]]')} />. Verify with the legs: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{3\\times4}{2}=6]]')} />. Both routes agree, as they must.</p>

  <h3>Example 3: An Isosceles Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[h=5]]')} />. Then <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{10\\times5}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=25}]]]')} />. The altitude equals half the hypotenuse, so the legs are equal and each acute angle is <span set:html={rich('[[45^\\circ]]')} />. This is the maximum possible area for any right triangle with hypotenuse 10.</p>

  <h3>Example 4: A Smaller Triangular Altitude</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c=13]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[h=5]]')} />. Then <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{13\\times5}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=32.5\\text{ square units}}]]]')} />. The altitude satisfies the limit because <span set:html={rich('[[5\\leq6.5]]')} />: half the hypotenuse is 6.5, and 5 is comfortably below it.</p>
</section>
<!-- Section 8: Twelve practical examples -->
<section id="sec-applications" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Real-Life Applications of Right Triangle Area From Hypotenuse and Altitude</h2>
  <p>Twelve worked situations show the formula at different scales and in different units. Each one names the triangle in the scene, gives the two measurements, substitutes step by step, and states the assumption behind it. Every example is a right-triangular panel, profile, section, or simplified model; the formula applies to the modelled triangle, not to every curved or irregular object of the same name.</p>

  <h3>Example 1: Triangular Glass Window</h3>
  <p>A right-triangular glass panel has a hypotenuse of <span set:html={rich('[[2.5\\text{ m}]]')} /> and a perpendicular altitude of <span set:html={rich('[[1.2\\text{ m}]]')} />. Using <span set:html={rich('[[K=ch/2]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{2.5\\times1.2}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=1.5\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} />. This is the flat glass area before the frame, assuming straight edges and a true right angle.</p>

  <h3>Example 2: Triangular Ramp Side Panel</h3>
  <p>A right-triangular side panel of a ramp has a hypotenuse of <span set:html={rich('[[4\\text{ m}]]')} /> and a perpendicular altitude of <span set:html={rich('[[1.5\\text{ m}]]')} />. Then <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{4\\times1.5}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=3\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} />. The result describes the triangular side profile, not the walking surface.</p>

  <h3>Example 3: Triangular Sail Section</h3>
  <p>A right-triangular sail section has a hypotenuse of <span set:html={rich('[[13\\text{ ft}]]')} /> and an altitude of <span set:html={rich('[[5\\text{ ft}]]')} />. Then <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{13\\times5}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=32.5\\text{ ft}^2}]]]')} />. This is the flat fabric area before seams or curvature; real sails billow, so the sail is modelled as flat.</p>

